Artille said:
There are several ways to structure it I believe, but I would say it should be pretty modular and easy to event however you like ^^
Click to expand...
in my case, I need to use the MapId's so it is used for randomizing 10 maps out of x maps (20~50).
every 5 maps a savepoint.
so I need to alter it anyway, as if it is to initilize it on part 1 (like on boot or first level, than change it on the other map).
but I need to add switches as well, so it can be ignored, else initilize a new group.
I might use a param struct if that is possible else a pre-made list and make it automatically with a simple scriptcall
to control it similar like:
JavaScript:
function initDungeons(complete, remain, ...dungeons) {
        var allDungeons = dungeons; //[1, 2, 3]; //Store as many as you need
        var completed = $gameVariables.value(complete) || []; //Will store the completed dungeons by ID (replace id 1 with what you need)
        var remaining = allDungeons.filter(function(id) {
            return !completed.includes(id); // This will add all id's from allDungeons to the list, except the ones present in completed
        });

        if (remaining.length > 0) { //If remaining has any entry left
            var nextDungeon = remaining[Math.floor(Math.random() * remaining.length)]; // Chose randomly amongst available id's
            $gameVariables.setValue(remain, nextDungeon); // Store the chosen dungeon ID in game variable 2 (replace id 2 with what you need)
        } else { //If no entries remain
            $gameMessage.add("You've completed all dungeons!"); //This is just for showing a message, replace with whatever you want to  happen
        }
    };


    function checkDungeon(complete, remain) {
        var completed = $gameVariables.value(complete); //Again, the same variable as previous script
        var dungeonID = $gameVariables.value(remain); //Same here, use the same ID as in the previous script
        if (!completed.includes(dungeonID)) completed.push(dungeonID); //This will add current dungeon to the array
        $gameVariables.setValue(complete, completed); //Updates the "completed" variable
    };
so it would be easier as a plugin, quick call, easy change values, without worries on typo's

